If you're polypiling potions or scrolls, blank/dilute them (with a wand of 
cancellation if you have one) and bless them all. Being blessed makes them 
last longer, and having them all the same type makes them easier to manage. 
Remember that you can combine polypiling with alchemy to get more of the good 
potions.

Use around 4 piles in a line. (For a wizard with a spellbook, use a very long 
line, since you don't want to put too much in a stack and waste items and you 
can always re-zap a pile that's out of range. Range is randomized.)

Don't let items form big stacks, like '20 blank scrolls'; instead, #name them 
in groups of one or two. That way you'll lose fewer at a time when they 
shudder, and you'll get to see more item types.

For armor and tools, magical items in give a much better chance of magical 
items out. Magic markers and unicorn horns are both considered magical.

You can get extremely good results polypiling properly separated blessed 
scrolls. If you repeatedly pick out enchant armor, enchant weapon, and 
charging then re-polymorh the rest until, 63% of them will come out as 
valuable scrolls.

